 <section id="math"><h2>Math functions</h2>
 <p>The equation parser is <b>math.js</b>, so all of its 220 documented functions work in equations. Each row shows the call forms and one example. The full manual is at <a href="https://mathjs.org/docs/reference/functions.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">mathjs.org</a>. Exceptions in equations: <code>evaluate</code>, <code>parse</code>, <code>compile</code>, <code>simplify</code>, <code>rationalize</code> and <code>resolve</code> work on formulas as text and are available only on the <a href="#consoleline">console command line</a>; <code>derivative(expression, x)</code> is worked out before solving (see <a href="#syntax">Equation syntax</a>). <code>createUnit</code>, <code>import</code> and <code>config</code> are not available anywhere, because they would change the settings of the program.</p>
-<h3 id="m-Added">Fits, roots, integration and statistics <small>(18)</small></h3><p>These functions are added by this program, with the same names and argument order as in Matlab. They take and return real numbers and arrays, and work in equations as well as on the console. A function that returns an array is indexed on the call: <code>c = polyfit(x, y, 2)[1]</code> (writing <code>p = polyfit(x, y, 2)</code> and then <code>p[1]</code> would make <code>p[1]</code> a separate unknown). See the examples <a href="#ex-m6">Fitting property data</a> and <a href="#ex-m7">Multiple regression</a>.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Function</th><th>Description and example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr class="item"><td><code>linspace(a, b)<br>linspace(a, b, n)</code></td><td>n equally spaced values from a to b (100 if n is left out).<br><span class="ex">e.g. <code>linspace(0, 1, 5)</code></span></td></tr><tr class="item"><td><code>logspace(a, b)<br>logspace(a, b, n)</code></td><td>n values from 10^a to 10^b, equally spaced on a log scale (50 if n is left out).<br><span class="ex">e.g. <code>logspace(0, 2, 3)</code></span></td></tr><tr class="item"><td><code>trapz(x, y)<br>trapz(y)</code></td><td>Trapezoid-rule integral of tabulated y over x (unit spacing if x is left out). For a formula, <code>integral()</code> is more accurate.<br><span class="ex">e.g. <code>trapz([0, 1, 3], [1, 2, 4])</code></span></td></tr><tr class="item"><td><code>cumtrapz(x, y)<br>cumtrapz(y)</code></td><td>Running trapezoid integral, starting at 0.<br><span class="ex">e.g. <code>cumtrapz([0, 1, 2], [0, 1, 4])</code></span></td></tr><tr class="item"><td><code>polyfit(x, y, n)<br>polyfit(x, y, n, 'stderr')<br>polyfit(x, y, n, 'r2')<br>polyfit(x, y, n, 'rmse')</code></td><td>Least-squares polynomial of degree n, coefficients highest power first. The last argument instead returns the standard errors of the coefficients, R<sup>2</sup>, or the rms residual with n - p degrees of freedom.<br><span class="ex">e.g. <code>polyfit([0, 1, 2, 3], [1, 3, 7, 13], 2)</code></span></td></tr><tr class="item"><td><code>regress(y, X)</code><br><code>regress(y, X, 'pvalue')</code> ...</td><td>Multiple linear regression y = X b, as in Matlab: one column of X per term, with a column of ones for a constant term (<code>X = matrixFromColumns(ones(n), x1, x2)</code>). Returns the coefficients b, or, with a last argument, the same statistics as polyfit (below).<br><span class="ex">e.g. <code>regress([1, 3, 5.1], [1, 0; 1, 1; 1, 2])</code></span></td></tr>
