lyfit([0, 1, 2, 3], [1, 3, 7, 13], 2)</code></span></td></tr><tr class="item"><td><code>regress(y, X)</code><br><code>regress(y, X, 'pvalue')</code> ...</td><td>Multiple linear regression y = X b, as in Matlab: one column of X per term, with a column of ones for a constant term (<code>X = matrixFromColumns(ones(n), x1, x2)</code>). Returns the coefficients b, or, with a last argument, the same statistics as polyfit (below).<br><span class="ex">e.g. <code>regress([1, 3, 5.1], [1, 0; 1, 1; 1, 2])</code></span></td></tr>
 <tr class="item"><td><code>'stderr'</code>, <code>'tstat'</code>, <code>'pvalue'</code>, <code>'ci'</code>, <code>'summary'</code>, <code>'r2'</code>, <code>'adjr2'</code>, <code>'rmse'</code>, <code>'resid'</code>, <code>'fit'</code></td><td>The last argument of polyfit, lsqfit and regress: standard errors of the parameters; t = parameter/standard error; the two-sided p-value of each parameter (below 0.05: significantly different from zero at the 5 % level; above: the data do not show that the term matters); confidence intervals, one row [low, high] per parameter (95 %, or the level given after it: <code>'ci', 0.99</code>); a summary with one row per parameter: value, standard error, t, p; R<sup>2</sup> and adjusted R<sup>2</sup>; the rms residual (with n - p degrees of freedom); the residuals y - fit; the fitted values. For lsqfit the statistics are those of the model linearized at the optimum, the usual approximation.<br><span class="ex">e.g. <code>polyfit([0, 1, 2, 3, 4], [1.1, 2.9, 5.2, 6.8, 9.1], 1, 'pvalue')</code></span></td></tr>
